<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Corneille</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1606">1606</a>–<a href="/years/1684">1684</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Corneille</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Corneille was a French dramatist, one of the three great playwrights of seventeenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moliere">Molière</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-racine">Racine</a>. His tragedy Le Cid of 1637 was a great success and set off a debate on the rules of drama in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academie-francaise">Académie française</a> took part. His plays, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> and Cinna, often show heroes torn between duty and passion, and he was elected to the Académie in 1647.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-corneille.jpg" alt="Pierre Corneille" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Charles Le Brun · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Corneille_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the seventeenth century Descartes, <b>Corneille</b>, Molière and Racine, the Académie française founded in 1635 and the court of Versailles made French taste the standard of European elites, and French increasingly replaced Latin as the language of diplomacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.44,1.1&amp;year=1656" aria-label="Rouen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4575 441 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pierre Corneille"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5025 711h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5025 711h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.44,1.1&amp;year=1656">What was here in 1656? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In conquering without danger we triumph without glory.&#8221;</p><footer>Le Cid (1637), act 2 &#183; tr. Roscoe Mongan, 1896 &#183; spoken by the Count, Chimène's father, to the young Rodrigo &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14954/pg14954-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;O rage! O despair! O inimical old age! Have I then lived so long only for this disgrace? And have I grown grey in warlike toils, only to see in one day so many of my laurels wither?&#8221;</p><footer>Le Cid (1637), act 1 &#183; tr. Roscoe Mongan, 1896 &#183; spoken by Don Diego, Rodrigo's father, after he has been struck &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14954/pg14954-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;He who fears not death fears not threats.&#8221;</p><footer>Le Cid (1637), act 2 &#183; tr. Roscoe Mongan, 1896 &#183; spoken by the Count, Chimène's father &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14954/pg14954-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
